          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $d,k\\ge1$, let $X\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be Borel, and give $X$ the relative Borel sigma algebra $\\mathcal B(X)=\\{X\\cap B:B\\text{ is in the sigma algebra generated by Euclidean open sets in }\\mathbb R^d\\}$. Let $\\mu$ be a probability measure on $\\mathcal B(X)$ with $\\int_X\\|x\\|_2^2\\,d\\mu(x)<\\infty$, and let $c_1,\\ldots,c_k\\in\\mathbb R^d$ be reproduction points, where $\\|x\\|_2^2:=\\sum_{\\ell=1}^d x_\\ell^2$. A finite squared-error quantizer is a measurable map $Q:X\\to\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}$, with the finite index set given its discrete sigma algebra; its reproduction at $x$ is $c_{Q(x)}$ and its distortion is\n\n$$D_\\mu(Q;c_1,\\ldots,c_k):=\\int_X\\|x-c_{Q(x)}\\|_2^2\\,d\\mu(x).$$\n\nThis is the expected single-letter distortion of [[def-it-single-letter-distortion-and-block-distortion]] for the measurable distortion $d(x,c)=\\|x-c\\|_2^2$. For a fixed ordered center tuple $c=(c_1,\\ldots,c_k)$, the nearest-center rule assigns $x$ to the least index minimizing $\\|x-c_j\\|_2^2$. Its cell at index $i$ is\n\n$$C_i(c):=X\\cap\\bigcap_{j<i}\\{x:\\|x-c_i\\|_2^2<\\|x-c_j\\|_2^2\\}\\cap\\bigcap_{j>i}\\{x:\\|x-c_i\\|_2^2\\le\\|x-c_j\\|_2^2\\}.$$\n\nThe convention assigns every tie to the smallest index. A general finite measurable quantizer need not use this nearest-center partition; in particular, a compander partition below is specified by its own interval boundaries. When $X=\\mathbb R^d$, this is the ordinary full-domain nearest-center quantizer.",
